Alembic Database Migrations
===========================

This directory contains database migration scripts for the Vaultline backend.

What Are Migrations?
--------------------
Migrations track changes to your database schema over time:
- Adding tables
- Adding/removing columns
- Changing data types
- Creating indexes
- Etc.

Directory Structure:
-------------------
alembic/
├── versions/          # Migration scripts (auto-generated)
├── env.py            # Alembic environment configuration
├── script.py.mako    # Template for new migrations
└── README            # This file

Common Commands:
----------------

1. Create a new migration (after changing models.py):
   alembic revision --autogenerate -m "Description of change"

2. Apply migrations (upgrade to latest):
   alembic upgrade head

3. Rollback one migration:
   alembic downgrade -1

4. See migration history:
   alembic history

5. Show current version:
   alembic current

Migration Workflow:
-------------------

1. Edit app/models.py (add/modify tables)
2. Generate migration: alembic revision --autogenerate -m "Added email to users"
3. Review the generated file in versions/
4. Apply migration: alembic upgrade head
5. Commit both models.py and the migration file to git

Example:
--------

# Add a new column to User model in models.py
class User(Base):
    ...
    phone_number = Column(String(20), nullable=True)

# Generate migration
$ alembic revision --autogenerate -m "Add phone number to users"
INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Context impl PostgresqlImpl.
INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Will assume transactional DDL.
INFO  [alembic.autogenerate.compare] Detected added column 'users.phone_number'
  Generating alembic\versions\2024_01_15_1234_add_phone_number_to_users.py ... done

# Review the generated file (important!)
# Then apply it
$ alembic upgrade head
INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Running upgrade abc123 -> def456, Add phone number to users

Production Deployment:
----------------------

Before deploying new code:
1. Backup the database!
2. Apply migrations: alembic upgrade head
3. Deploy new application code
4. Verify everything works

If something goes wrong:
1. Rollback code deployment
2. Rollback migration: alembic downgrade -1
3. Restore database backup if needed

Safety Tips:
------------
- Always backup before migrations
- Review auto-generated migrations (they're not perfect!)
- Test migrations on staging first
- Keep migrations small and focused
- Never edit applied migrations (create new ones)
- Commit migrations with related code changes

Troubleshooting:
----------------

Error: "Can't locate revision identified by 'abc123'"
Solution: Delete alembic/versions/*.py and regenerate from scratch

Error: "Target database is not up to date"
Solution: Run: alembic upgrade head

Error: "Table 'users' already exists"
Solution: You might need to: alembic stamp head (to mark current state)
